<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marañón River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Marañón is the main headwater of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a>, rising in the Andes of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and flowing about 1,700 km north-west and then east through deep canyons before joining the Ucayali to form the Amazon proper. Its valleys bordered the homeland of the Chachapoya culture. The river was long regarded by Spanish geographers as the true source of the Amazon.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maranon-river.jpg" alt="Marañón River" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gerd Breitenbach · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mara%C3%B1%C3%B3n_River_in_2003.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Marañón rises about 160 kilometres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> and runs north-west across plateaus about 3,650 metres high, through a deeply eroded Andean valley along the eastern base of the Cordillera. At about 5° 36′ south it bends north-east and cuts through the high jungle, where unnavigable rapids and gorges called pongos mark its course.</p><p>The last is the Pongo de Manseriche, about 5 kilometres long, which in 1868 Captain Carbajal described as a rent in the Andes about 600 metres deep and in places only 30 metres wide, with a current at times near 20 kilometres an hour. It has wrecked many ships. Below it the river enters the flat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> basin, the low jungle of Peruvian Amazonia, home to the Urarina, Candoshi and Cocama-Cocamilla peoples.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Marañón was considered the source of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> from the 1707 map of Padre Samuel Fritz, who placed its source in a lake called Lauriocha, near Huánuco, and judged that it contributed more water than any other tributary. For most of the 18th and 19th centuries and into the 20th it was generally held to be the source.</p><p>Later exploration proposed two headwater rivers, the Lauricocha and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nupe-kingdom">Nupe</a>, which join near the village of Rondos. The Apurímac and Mantaro have also been put forward as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a>'s source, but the Marañón is still called its mainstem source because of its higher annual discharge. Historically the name was often applied to the whole river to the Atlantic, whereas it is now generally taken to end at the confluence with the Ucayali.</p><p>In 1743 the Frenchman Charles Marie de La Condamine travelled from the Chinchipe confluence to the Atlantic, taking to the water at the Chiriaco confluence and running several pongos, including the Pongo de Manseriche. In 1941 and 1942 Bertrand Flornoy, sent by the National Museum of Natural History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, found the sources of the river.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>A 552-kilometre section between Puente Copuma and Corral Quemado, much of it in a canyon up to 3,000 metres deep and in dry country, has been called the Grand Canyon of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a>. It flows past the village of Calemar, where Ciro Alegría set his novel La serpiente de oro of 1935. The river has also been the subject of plans for hydroelectric dams, one of which, at Chaglla, was begun in 2012.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marañón River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Chachapoya culture developed from around 800 in the montane forests between the <b>Marañón</b> and Huallaga rivers, in present-day Amazonas region of Peru.</blockquote><a href="/state/chachapoya">Chachapoya</a></li></ul></section>
